**What**
An entitys identity is a human-invented ref path segment. `Store::store` makes
the caller supply one; the CLI defaults it to the kind name;
`Store::store_anonymous` derives one from the first 8 hex characters of the
commit id. That third method is unreachable - it is not called from the CLI,
not mentioned in any README, and not in `docs/specification.adoc`.

**Where**
`crates/gix-store/src/store.rs:148-184` (`store_anonymous`, the only occurrence
of that identifier anywhere in the repository),
`crates/git-store/src/main.rs:203` (`let name = name.as_deref().unwrap_or(&kind)`).

**Why it matters**
The comment calls it a "chicken-and-egg": you cannot name a thing by its
content hash before you have written it. But the workaround has three problems
that suggest the gap is upstream of the workaround.

1. It is *not* content-addressed. The name comes from the **commit** id, which
   includes author and timestamp, so storing byte-identical content twice
   yields two different names. The one property auto-naming should have -
   idempotence - is absent.
2. An 8-hex-character prefix is 32 bits. On collision the
   `PreviousValue::MustNotExist` reference call fails with a raw `gix` error
   collapsed into `Error::Git`, with no retry and no diagnosable message.
3. It is dead. Shipping an unexercised public method on the librarys main
   type, in a repository being presented as a design artifact, invites the
   question of why it is there.

The deeper issue is that identity is the one thing the schema does not
describe. `SchemaDoc` says what fields a `recipe` has; it does not say what
makes two recipes the same recipe. So identity leaks upward into the CLI, where
`git store put recipe` with no name silently overwrites the single entity
called `recipe` - the README calls this "one canonical entity per kind", which
is a reasonable default and also a surprising one for a command that writes.
Every entity-oriented store this resembles - a document DB, a Git-backed issue
tracker, a CRDT store - answers "what is the key" in the schema, not at the
call site.

**Options**
- Option A - delete `store_anonymous` and keep names explicit. Honest, smallest
  diff, and the model becomes "the ref name is the primary key, you choose it".
  Trade-off: gives up on auto-naming entirely.
- Option B - let the schema declare an identity: `SchemaDoc { root, defs,
  identity: Option<Vec<String>> }` naming one or more field paths. `store` then
  derives the name from the values of those fields, so a recipe with
  `title: "Carbonara"` lands at `refs/store/recipe/carbonara` deterministically
  and re-storing it is an update, not a duplicate. Trade-off: semver-major on
  the schema format, and it needs a slug/escape rule to fit the ref-name
  constraints `refname.rs` already enforces.
- Option C - fix `store_anonymous` to derive the name from the **tree** id
  rather than the commit id, making it genuinely content-addressed and
  idempotent, and expose it as `git store put <kind>` with no name. Trade-off:
  content-addressed names mean an edit creates a *new* entity rather than a new
  version, which is a different data model than the rest of the system.

**Recommendation**
Option B is the right shape and it is a good slide: "the schema tells you what
a thing is *and* which thing it is". If that is too large before the talk, do
Option A - deleting an unused method is better than shipping a half-answer to a
question the audience will notice you noticed. Medium-high confidence: the
existence of `store_anonymous` is itself the evidence that the naming model is
incomplete, and patching around it with a fourth method would be the wrong
move.